    "source": { "id": "148:2149", "name": "Button", "type": "COMPONENT_SET" },
    "description": "The single button component for the system: 3 intents, 3 emphasis levels, 3 sizes, 5 interaction states, and optional leading/trailing icons — 135 variants in one set.\nPURPOSE: Every actionable control that carries a text label. intent communicates meaning (brand for the product's primary path, strong for actions with no brand or destructive weight, subtle for supplementary actions). emphasis communicates priority within a screen region (primary for the single most important action, secondary for supporting actions, tertiary for incidental ones in dense surfaces).\nBEHAVIOR: The state axis documents default, hover, focus, active and disabled. These are presentation states, not a runtime prop — see CODE GENERATION NOTES. The focus variant adds a dedicated \"Focus ring\" rectangle offset -4px from the button bounds, bound to border/focus and border-width/focus.\nCOMPOSITION: Primary emphasis is a solid fill with an inverse label. Secondary is a light surface with a 1px intent-colored border and matching label. Tertiary has no fill or border — label only, with a background wash on hover and pressed. All sizes use corner-radius/rounded. Optional Icon instances sit either side of the label, toggled by the iconLeading and iconTrailing booleans; label text is the text property.\nUSAGE: One primary-emphasis action per screen region; everything else steps down to secondary or tertiary. Do not mix intents within a button group to signal hierarchy — change emphasis instead, and change intent only when the meaning changes.\nACCESSIBILITY: Renders as &lt;button&gt;. Keyboard: reachable via Tab, activated by Enter and Space. Implement the focus ring as outline with outline-offset on :focus-visible, never as a layout-affecting border. The state variants are visual documentation, not props: hover/focus/pressed/disabled map to CSS pseudo-classes and the disabled attribute. A disabled button must still convey its label to assistive tech; prefer aria-disabled over removing it from the tab order when the reason for disablement is not obvious. Icon-only usage is not supported here — use the Icon Button family, which requires an accessible name.\nCODE GENERATION NOTES: This maps to ONE Button component. intent (brand | subtle | strong) and emphasis (primary | secondary | tertiary) are independent props that select tokens; do not emit a component or class per combination. size maps to l/m/s (56/44/36px heights). Do not emit state as a prop. Bind every color to the named token rather than a literal hex so Light and Dark modes all resolve. iconLeading and iconTrailing are optional ReactNode slots — reuse the existing Icon component rather than inlining SVG. A Button can only have one icon at most at any given time. A button must never have both iconLeading and iconTrailing at the same time.",
